    "body": "© Artur Szczybylo\nBy Alexander Whiteman\n24 November 2026\nShippers risk undermining their supply chain resilience by awarding transport contracts primarily on price, with increasingly little protection offered by logistics operators when costs rise or capacity constraints hinder movements.\nChris Clowes, executive director of Scala, said while companies often sought to diversify transport suppliers to reduce exposure to disruption, that approach could create a false sense of security if providers are operating on commercially unsustainable rates.\nHe told The Loadstar : “Having two transport providers on a particular lane may look like sensible contingency planning, but if both have won business by bidding at unsustainably low rates in a procurement exercise, how resilient is the arrangement?\n“What happens when costs increase, or capacity becomes constrained? Logistics decision-makers should consider the commercial sustainability of suppliers, as well as their operational capability.”\nRather than judging resilience on the number of suppliers they use, Mr Clowes said shippers needed to assess just how genuinely independent and commercially viable their alternative arrangements were.\nHe argued that procurement decisions should account for the financial sustainability of logistics providers alongside their operational capabilities, noting the issue reflects a wider problem with how companies approach supply chain resilience.\nThis, he said was evident in the tendency to concentrate on individual risks rather than examining dependencies that could undermine contingency arrangements. And he pointed out that having multiple suppliers mattered little if they were exposed to the same disruption.\n“Having two suppliers isn’t necessarily a resilient solution if they are both in the same location and that region experiences a prolonged drought, because while you have diversified your suppliers, you have not addressed the underlying risk,” he explained.\n“Resilience means understanding where the real risks and dependencies sit, testing whether contingency arrangements are genuinely independent, and ensuring that alternative arrangements are commercially and operationally viable.”\nDespite increased awareness of supply chain risks, Mr Clowes said he was concerned that too many companies continued to respond to disruption as it happened, rather than testing whether their supply chains could withstand different scenarios.\nHowever, he said if they were able to ensure the resilience of their own business model, smaller forwarders had an opportunity to differentiate themselves by offering reliable service, based on flexibile and strong customer relations.\n“Smaller operators can often be more agile and responsive than larger organisations – particularly where they have strong local knowledge and established relationships. That flexibility has real commercial value,” he added.",
